Post-hoc reasoning and the illusion of transparency
2026-11-20

You have been in the following situation:

> You: Why did this system produce this weird number?
>
> Coworker: Oh, it's because of this code in this file that implements the
> Flumpus Convention.

Later on:

> You: I tried to do this other thing and it doesn't fit the Flumpus Convention.
>
> Coworker: I must have been wrong, let me check again. Oh yeah, it's because of
> this other code implementing the Wumpus Convention, my bad.

This is frustrating but code is code, it's sometimes hard to understand. It's a
common problem - people do not remember how code works and come up with some
kind of rationalization. When checked, those rationalizations don't hold up.

Later, you ask your coworker about their own thought processes:

> You: What was your thought process when deciding between the Flumpus and
> Wumpus Conventions?
>
> Coworker: Oh, that's easy, I did it this way because of this requirement we
> had at the time to go Wumpus.
>
> You: But the initial commit to this file actually calls into flumpus.cpp?
>
> Coworker: Oh my bad, yes, that was it, we converted to Wumpus last week.

This is a common situation - people do not remember and cannot reliably
reproduce their thought processes, yet come up with post-hoc rationalizations
anyway. When those rationalizations are checked, they often do not hold up.

These are two instances of the same problem - your coworker believes they have
enough information to answer your question, but they fundamentally cannot.

* I like the statement of [Rice's
  theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice%27s_theorem) - all nontrivial
  semantic properties of programs are undecidable. This does not mean that
  _every_ program is impervious to reading it and understanding it, but most
  programs cannot be understood without running them. Your coworker tried to
  decide a semantic property of the program without running it.

* Your coworker did not ever store or record their thought processes. Even if
  they tried to, this would usually take the form of writing notes _after_
  having thoughts - there is no known reliable mechanism we have of recording
  thoughts, and this includes human memory. It is questionable whether humans
  even have access to their own thought processes.

The second claim is spicier, but these statements are actually very closely
related if you regard your coworker themselves as a program we are trying to
inspect - they do not have a privileged position when it comes to inspecting
their own thought processes. Even worse - there is no way to run the program
on selected inputs and check your hypotheses about its thoughts.

